        voices: {
            kicker: 'VOICES · THE TOWN ANSWERS',
            headline: '“I Teach in a Building Designed to Produce Punctual Employees and I Am Asked Why the Bell Bothers Them.”',
            standfirst: 'Two questions went round Tonio Town this week: what school was actually built to do, and whether the people who change a field are ever the people it trained. The staff room answered.',
            turns: [
                {
                    q: 'The article says the classroom was designed to resemble a workplace.',
                    a: 'Look at my room. Rows, a clock, a bell, a person at the front with the authority, and a document that says what everyone will be doing at eleven. I am not complaining, exactly. That design taught more people to read than anything in history and I would not trade it for a fantasy. But I would like to stop hearing that it is the natural shape of learning. It is the shape of a nineteenth-century plan that worked.',
                },
                {
                    q: 'The law created adolescence as a category.',
                    a: 'And nobody says that out loud in this profession, which is strange, because it explains my entire job. Put every fifteen-year-old in one building, all day, for years, and the peer group becomes the most powerful force in their life — more than the family, much more than me. That is not a failure of parenting. That is a structural consequence of the timetable, and we designed it, and then we act astonished by it every September.',
                },
                {
                    q: 'And the designer who never trained?',
                    a: 'I have three former students who are excellent at something nobody taught them and one who was ruined by being good at school. What that story tells me is not that training is worthless. It is that training gives you the field’s existing answers, and the people who change a field are usually the ones who did not receive them and had to construct something to fill the gap. My job is to hand out the existing answers. I try to remember what that costs.',
                },
                {
                    q: 'Would you have let her into your class?',
                    a: 'I would have loved her and I would have marked her down, which is the honest and horrible answer. Everything I am required to grade rewards a student who explains their reasoning in the accepted format. She reportedly refuses to explain her work at all, to anyone, on principle. That would be a C in my class with a comment about developing the argument, and she has an exhibition at the Metropolitan Museum.',
                },
            ],
        },
        story: {
            kicker: 'FICTION · TONIO TOWN',
            headline: 'The Third Draft',
            cast: 'Ms. Reyes · Jayden · a rubric that did not fit the work',
            byline: 'A Tonio Times Original',
            body: [
                'Jayden runs things. He has run the sophomore fundraiser, the equipment shed, the sound desk and, briefly and disastrously, a car wash. He does not run essays.',
                'The assignment was four pages on a text of his choosing. What he handed in was a page and a half, in a form Ms. Reyes had not seen before, laid out as a set of instructions.',
                'It was, in every way the rubric could measure, incomplete.',
                'It was also the only piece in twenty-six papers that had told her something she did not already know.',
                '“This is not the assignment,” she said, after class.',
                '“I know.”',
                '“It is a page and a half.”',
                '“I know.”',
                '“Why did you write it like a manual?”',
                'Jayden shrugged in the way that means he has an answer and has learned that the answer usually costs him.',
                '“Because that is how I understand things. I built it so somebody could do it. You can do what is in there. You cannot do a five-paragraph essay.”',
                'Ms. Reyes looked at the rubric, which she did not write, and which is used by four teachers and matches a state form.',
                '“I have to grade this against that,” she said, “and it will not do well.”',
                '“I know.”',
                '“So here is what will happen. You will get the grade the form gives you. And I am going to photocopy this and take it to the department in October, because the fastest way to change a form is to be holding something it cannot measure.”',
                'Jayden took the paper back and looked at the grade for a while.',
                '“Is that a good deal for me?”',
                '“No,” said Ms. Reyes. “It is a good deal for the next one.”',
                '— The department met in October. The rubric now has a line for alternative forms, worth four points out of forty. Ms. Reyes calls this a disgrace and a victory, depending on the day.',
            ],
        },
