        quote: {
            text: 'Who owns the patent on this vaccine? Well, the people, I would say. Could you patent the sun?',
            source: 'Jonas Salk, interviewed by Edward R. Murrow, 1955',
        },
        lead: {
            kicker: 'THE LONG VIEW · SAFETY',
            headline: 'The Car Did Not Become Safe. The Car Was Made Safe, Against Objections, in Public',
            standfirst: 'For sixty years the industry’s position was that crashes are caused by drivers. The counter-argument was that people will always crash, so the question is what the inside of the car does next — and it took a book, an act and thirty years of law to win.',
            byline: 'By the Long View Desk',
            body: [
                'The three-point belt was designed by Nils Bohlin, an engineer at Volvo who had come from ejection seats, and fitted as standard from 1959. Earlier belts were two-point lap straps that restrained the hips and let the upper body fold, and the innovation was geometry: a single strap crossing the chest and the pelvis, anchored at three points, which a person could fasten with one hand. Volvo then did the thing the story is actually about. It opened the patent, making the design available to other manufacturers rather than holding it as an advantage, on the reasoning that a safety device is worth more in every car than in one brand. Almost nobody in any industry does this. It is the reason the belt in your car is that shape.',
                'The American fight was slower and nastier because it was about liability rather than engineering. In 1965 Ralph Nader published Unsafe at Any Speed, arguing that manufacturers designed cars that killed people in survivable crashes and knew it; General Motors responded by hiring investigators to follow him, was caught, and its president apologised to a Senate subcommittee — an own goal so complete that it made the book a national event. The National Traffic and Motor Vehicle Safety Act followed in 1966, creating the federal agency that could set standards, and belts were required in new American cars from 1968. Required to be installed. Nobody was required to wear one.',
                'That took another sixteen years and a change in the argument. Use laws were fought on the ground that wearing a belt was a personal choice with personal consequences; the counter was that the consequences are public, since the cost falls on emergency services, insurers and families. New York passed the first American mandatory-use law in 1984 and the rest of the country followed state by state, each one with the same debate. Airbags, which the industry had resisted since the 1970s, were mandated in all new cars by the late 1990s. Every one of these measures was described at the time as an unreasonable imposition and is now invisible.',
                'The numbers are the whole argument. American road deaths peaked at around fifty-four thousand in 1972 and have run at something closer to forty thousand in recent years, over a period in which the population grew by more than half and the number of miles driven roughly doubled. The death rate per mile has fallen by something like three quarters. None of that came from better drivers — there is no evidence that anybody drives better than their grandparents did — and almost all of it came from the interior of the car, the structure around it, and a law that arrived, in each case, some years after the engineering was already available and cheap.',
            ],
        },
        rail: {
            kicker: 'THE GREEN ROOM · SCIENCE',
            headline: 'He Tested It on Himself, His Wife and His Three Sons Before He Tested It on Anybody Else',
            byline: 'By the Green Room Desk',
            body: [
                'Polio in the early 1950s was the disease American parents were most afraid of, and the fear was not irrational: it arrived in summer, it took children, and nobody could explain why one street had cases and the next did not. Swimming pools closed. Cinemas emptied. Jonas Salk, working at Pittsburgh with money raised largely from the public through a foundation built on small donations, developed a vaccine using killed virus — a route that senior figures in the field regarded as inferior to a live attenuated approach and said so, loudly, for years. Before any public trial he inoculated himself, his wife and their three sons.',
                'The field trial of 1954 remains one of the largest medical experiments ever run: something close to two million children took part, with a double-blind placebo-controlled core, organised by volunteers in schools across the country. The results were announced on April 12, 1955, and the reaction was not medical, it was civic — church bells were rung, factories stopped, strangers wept in the street. Within a few years the number of American cases fell by more than ninety per cent. Albert Sabin’s oral live vaccine, easier to administer and giving broader community protection, arrived shortly afterwards and became the global workhorse; the two men disagreed about each other’s methods for the rest of their lives.',
                'The line that survives came from a television interview. Asked who owned the patent, Salk said, in effect, that the people did, and asked whether you could patent the sun. Lawyers for the foundation had in fact looked at whether the vaccine could be patented and concluded it probably could not have been, which is the sort of footnote that people use to spoil the story; it does not spoil it, because he had every opportunity to take the credit and the money by other routes and did not, and there is no version of his life in which he tried. He never received a Nobel Prize. He built an institute in California instead and spent his last decades on other problems, unbothered, being the second most decorated man in his own field and the only one whose name a parent could recall thirty years later.',
            ],
        },
