        voices: {
            kicker: 'VOICES · THE TOWN ANSWERS',
            headline: '“I Have Watched a Rule Nobody Can Change Get Changed Every Year by People Explaining It.”',
            standfirst: 'Two questions went round Tonio Town this week: whether an old document is wisdom or inertia, and how one person alters a rule. The HOA answered, at length, in writing, as it does everything.',
            turns: [
                {
                    q: 'You maintain a rulebook. Is a hard-to-change rulebook a good thing?',
                    a: 'Yes, and I am the last man in this county who will be believed saying it, so let me give the honest version. Our covenant needs two-thirds of the households to amend. That is why we still have a rule about aerials that has been meaningless since 2009. It is also why nobody has been able to ban colours, or dogs, or the Ramirez boy’s van, in the three separate years when a loud majority wanted to. A slow rulebook protects you from your own worst summer.',
                },
                {
                    q: 'The article says the meaning moves even when the text does not.',
                    a: 'That is what I actually spend my time on and it is uncomfortable to admit. Nobody amends anything. What happens is that I get asked what a word means, in a specific case, on a Thursday, and whatever I answer becomes the rule, and the next person cites me instead of the document. Eleven years of that and the covenant says one thing on paper and another thing in practice, and I could not tell you the day it changed.',
                },
                {
                    q: 'And the student who spent ten years on the amendment?',
                    a: 'C grade, and he was right. I want that framed. But the part everyone skips is the ten years of letters. He did not win the argument in 1982, he won it in 1992, one legislature at a time, at his own cost, in envelopes. There is no shortcut in that story and it is why nobody tells it properly. It is a story about postage.',
                },
                {
                    q: 'And the man who fixed basketball with a division?',
                    a: 'That is the one I will be using at the next meeting, and Donna will hate it. He did not write a philosophy of the game. He counted what a good game looked like and divided by the clock. If we had done that with the parking rules instead of arguing about fairness for three months, we would have had a number in an evening. Principles produce meetings. Quotients produce rules.',
                },
            ],
        },
        story: {
            kicker: 'FICTION · TONIO TOWN',
            headline: 'Section Four, Paragraph Two',
            cast: 'Hank · Donna · a rule about the wrong thing',
            byline: 'A Tonio Times Original',
            body: [
                'The covenant says that no vehicle may be parked on the street overnight between the first of November and the first of April. It says this because of snow ploughs, in 1988, when the town still owned two.',
                'The town now owns one, contracts the rest, and ploughs the street at four in the morning regardless, around whatever is there.',
                'Donna wants the rule enforced. Donna has wanted the rule enforced since the Ramirez van.',
                '“It is the rule,” said Donna.',
                '“It is,” said Hank.',
                '“So enforce it.”',
                'Hank took out the covenant, which he carries, and read section four aloud, all of it, including the part nobody has read since 1988, which begins: for the purpose of permitting municipal snow removal.',
                '“For the purpose of,” he said.',
                '“That is preamble.”',
                '“It is a purpose clause. The rule has a stated purpose. The purpose is a plough that no longer comes.”',
                'Donna, who has run the block party for nine years and has never lost a procedural argument in this county, went quiet for a moment.',
                '“Then amend it.”',
                '“Two-thirds of the households,” said Hank. “Which we will not get, in November, over a van.”',
                '“So the rule stands and you will not enforce it.”',
                '“The rule stands,” said Hank, “and I will enforce it the first winter the plough comes back. That is not me being clever. That is the sentence the men in 1988 actually wrote.”',
                '— Donna has since read the whole covenant twice, looking for the next purpose clause. She has found four. She has not told Hank about three of them.',
            ],
        },
