        voices: {
            kicker: 'VOICES · THE TOWN ANSWERS',
            headline: '“I Have Carried the Same Booklet Through Four Countries and It Has Never Once Described Me.”',
            standfirst: 'Two questions went round Tonio Town this week: what a document is actually for, and what it costs to start again in a language you did not grow up in. The gate answered, and so did the arrivals hall.',
            turns: [
                {
                    q: 'You work a departure gate. What do you actually see in a passport?',
                    a: 'I see whether the machine says yes. That is the honest answer and it took me a while to be able to say it without softening it. I do not read the person. I read a chip and a face and a colour of cover, and the colour of the cover decides how long the next twenty minutes take, for reasons that have nothing to do with the human being in front of me. I have watched two people with identical plans get completely different mornings, and I was the one who handed them out.',
                },
                {
                    q: 'The article says the booklet was meant to be temporary.',
                    a: 'Everything at an airport was meant to be temporary. The shoes, the liquids, the extra line, every single one of them was a measure for one year. Nothing in my industry has ever been removed. That is not a conspiracy, it is worse, it is inertia. Removing a control means being the person whose name is on the memo if anything ever happens. So the controls stay, forever, and are added to.',
                },
                {
                    q: 'And the writer who changed language at forty?',
                    a: 'Everyone tells that story as bravery. I am going to tell it as arithmetic, because I am on the other side of it. Changing language does not mean learning words. It means agreeing that for about ten years you will be less funny, less precise and less intelligent than you actually are, in front of people who will simply believe what they see. He was already famous in the first language, and he still did it. I was not famous in mine and I nearly did not.',
                },
                {
                    q: 'Do the two pieces meet?',
                    a: 'They meet on the question of what proves you. The booklet proves you to a state, and the language proves you to a room, and neither one is you. The interesting people are the ones who found that out early and kept going anyway. And the sentence in your paper I will be repeating is the one about a shared grammar for saying no. That is my building. I work in the grammar.',
                },
            ],
        },
        story: {
            kicker: 'FICTION · TONIO TOWN',
            headline: 'The Middle Name',
            cast: 'Hana · Denise (gate agent) · a name that would not fit the field',
            byline: 'A Tonio Times Original',
            body: [
                'The system at gate B12 allows twenty-four characters for a name. Hana’s legal name, with both family names and the middle one her grandmother chose, is twenty-nine.',
                'It has been twenty-nine for as long as she has been alive and it has been twenty-four on every ticket she has ever held.',
                '“It does not match,” said Denise, at the desk, at six in the morning, looking at the booklet and then the screen and then the booklet.',
                '“It never matches.”',
                '“I have to say the words. It does not match.”',
                '“I know the words,” said Hana. “I have heard the words in three countries.”',
                'Denise put the passport down. Behind Hana there were forty people and a boarding time.',
                '“Say your full name.”',
                'Hana said it. All twenty-nine characters, in the order her grandmother put them, at a normal speed, which took about two seconds.',
                '“Again, slower.”',
                'She said it again, slower.',
                'Denise typed something that took much longer than a name, printed a new boarding pass, and handed both back through the gap in the glass.',
                '“It still does not match,” she said. “I have written what it is short by, and where. Nobody downstream will stop you now. They will see the note before they see the gap.”',
                'Hana looked at the pass. Her name was still cut off at twenty-four characters.',
                '“It is the same,” she said.',
                '“The paper is the same,” said Denise. “What is different is that somebody wrote down that the paper was wrong.”',
                '— Denise has left that note on eleven passengers in six years. She has never been asked about it, and she has never once been able to make the field longer.',
            ],
        },
