        lead: {
            kicker: 'THE LONG VIEW · LITERACY',
            headline: 'Reading Went From a Minority Skill to a Human Default in About Seventy Years',
            standfirst: 'It is the fastest change in human capability on record and it happened almost entirely inside the lifetime of people still alive. Nobody marks it, because the winners of it are busy reading.',
            byline: 'By the Long View Desk',
            body: [
                'For most of recorded history, writing was a professional instrument rather than a general one. It was operated by scribes, clerics and administrators, in scripts that took years to learn, and its difficulty was not always accidental — a system that takes a decade to master is a system with a guild attached. The clearest evidence that this was a choice rather than a necessity is Korea. In the 1440s King Sejong commissioned and promulgated a new alphabet, Hangul, explicitly designed so that an ordinary person could learn it quickly, and the preface says as much: that the common people, wishing to speak, could not express themselves in the borrowed Chinese characters. Sections of the literate class opposed it for exactly the reason you would expect. It was easy.',
                'Europe’s version came through the press and the pulpit. Movable type in the middle of the fifteenth century cut the cost of a copy by orders of magnitude, and the Reformation supplied the motive to use it, since a doctrine that requires each believer to read scripture requires each believer to read. Luther’s German Bible in the 1520s and 30s was a mass-market product that also fixed a standard written language, and the Protestant regions of Europe show measurably higher literacy for centuries afterwards. Reading spread not because societies valued the mind but because two institutions, commerce and religion, each needed a population that could receive instructions without a middleman.',
                'The global change is startlingly recent. At the middle of the twentieth century, the best estimates available to UNESCO put something like half the world’s adults outside literacy altogether; today the figure is around one adult in eight, with the remaining illiteracy concentrated among older people, rural populations and, disproportionately, women. UNESCO declared an International Literacy Day in 1966 precisely because the change looked achievable rather than utopian. Individual campaigns show how fast it can move when a state decides it is a priority: Cuba’s literacy year in 1961 sent a hundred thousand young volunteers into the countryside for months and claimed near-universal basic literacy at the end of it — a figure disputed in its details and undeniable in its direction.',
                'What remains is the harder half, and it is not a headline. Being able to decode a sentence is not the same as being able to read a lease, a dosage instruction, a ballot or a contract, and functional literacy of that kind is weak in wealthy countries that record themselves as fully literate. There is no country on earth where the ability to read has been distributed evenly. There is now, for the first time, no serious country that argues it should not be.',
            ],
        },
        rail: {
            kicker: 'THE RECORD · HISTORY',
            headline: 'He Learned the Alphabet Because Somebody Warned His Owner Not to Teach Him',
            byline: 'By the Record Desk',
            body: [
                'Frederick Douglass told the story himself, repeatedly, because it is the whole argument in one scene. As a boy enslaved in Baltimore, he was being taught his letters by the wife of the household, who did not at first understand what she was doing wrong. Her husband stopped it, and explained, within the child’s hearing, that education would forever unfit a slave to be a slave — that a man who could read would become discontented and unmanageable. Douglass understood immediately that he had been handed the plan. The prohibition told him precisely where the exit was, and he later wrote that from that moment he understood the pathway from slavery to freedom.',
                'He assembled the skill out of fragments, trading bread to poor white boys in the street for reading lessons, copying letters from timber marked at the shipyard, working through a schoolbook of speeches he had bought with his own coins — a collection of orations that gave a teenage boy in bondage the argumentative architecture of the age. He escaped north in 1838 disguised as a sailor and carrying identification papers belonging to another man, which is a detail worth pairing with any discussion of documents: the first thing freedom required was somebody else’s paperwork.',
                'His Narrative appeared in 1845 and was so specific, so full of real names and places, that it endangered him; he left for Britain and Ireland, where audiences paid to hear him and supporters raised the money that bought his legal manumission in 1846. He came back, founded his own newspaper, The North Star, in 1847 under the line that right is of no sex, truth is of no colour, and spent the following decades as the most formidable public speaker in the country — advising presidents, breaking with allies, arguing with abolitionists who wanted him to stay a witness rather than become a thinker. His answer to that pressure is the durable part. He declined to be the evidence in someone else’s case.',
            ],
        },
