        voices: {
            kicker: 'VOICES · THE TOWN ANSWERS',
            headline: '“They Gave Us the Monday. Ask Anybody in This Room What It Is For.”',
            standfirst: 'Two questions went round Tonio Town this week: what a holiday is compensation for, and what it costs to say no at the exact moment saying yes is worth the most. The bar answered on a day off nobody could explain.',
            turns: [
                {
                    q: 'Does anybody here know what the holiday commemorates?',
                    a: 'Nobody, and I asked eleven people at the counter before you got here, and the best answer I got was “the workers,” which is not an answer, it is a shrug with a noun in it. And I am not being superior. I did not know either until I was forty. Here is the part that stays with me. It was signed in the middle of the strike. That is not a tribute. That is a man handing you a coat while his people are still in your street.',
                },
                {
                    q: 'Is it wrong to enjoy it?',
                    a: 'Absolutely not, and anybody who tells you otherwise wants your Monday. Take the day. Take every day they hand you. But know what you are holding. A concession is not an insult, it is a receipt. Somebody paid for that Monday in 1894 and the payment was not made in money, and once a year I think it is reasonable to know the name of the man who went to prison for it.',
                },
                {
                    q: 'And the fighter who refused, at his peak?',
                    a: 'The number that ends the argument is three and a half years. Not the speeches, not the poetry. Twenty-five to twenty-eight, gone, for a boxer. Everybody in a bar says they would stand on principle, and they are all imagining standing on it for an afternoon. He stood on it for the best part of a decade of earnings, when he was the most hated man in the country, before anybody clapped. The clapping came fifteen years later and cost nothing.',
                },
                {
                    q: 'The paper says the court ruled on procedure, not conscience.',
                    a: 'Which is the most Tuesday thing I have ever heard and I love it. The great moral confrontation of the age was settled because a board did not write down its reasons. That is how institutions actually lose. Not to a better argument — to their own paperwork. And it is worth remembering when somebody tells you that the truth always comes out. It does not. Occasionally a form is filled in badly and the truth gets a lift.',
                },
            ],
        },
        story: {
            kicker: 'FICTION · TONIO TOWN',
            headline: 'The Cookout Nobody Called',
            cast: 'Dave Boyd · Tammy · Reggie · a grill lit on a holiday',
            byline: 'A Tonio Times Original',
            body: [
                'The Boyds have hosted the September cookout for eleven years and this year, for the first time, nobody put it on the calendar.',
                'It happened anyway. By eleven there were nine people in the yard and by one there were twenty-six, and nobody could name the person who had started it.',
                '“Somebody told somebody,” said Tammy.',
                '“That is not a chain of events,” said Dave, who had lit the grill at nine on the theory that if it was not happening, he would simply be a man cooking for his family, which is not embarrassing.',
                'Reggie arrived at two with a folding chair and no food, as he has for eleven years.',
                '“What are we observing?” said Reggie.',
                '“The holiday.”',
                '“Which is for what?”',
                'Dave turned a piece of chicken. The yard, which had been loud, was suddenly a room in which twenty-six adults were discovering simultaneously that they did not know.',
                '“The working man,” said somebody, eventually.',
                '“Which working man?”',
                '“Reggie,” said Tammy, “eat something.”',
                '“I will eat,” said Reggie, “and I will note that we have organised a party for a reason none of us can state, on a day chosen by a Congress that was, at the time, extremely busy.”',
                'He sat down in the good spot under the tree, which he had identified in 2015 and has occupied every year since.',
                '“That said,” he added, “this is the best chicken you have done.”',
                'Dave did not say anything for a moment, because Dave has been waiting for a sentence like that since the brisket.',
                '— Twenty-six people, no invitation, and the only man who complained arrived first and left last. Tammy has written next year’s date on the calendar in pen.',
            ],
        },
