        lead: {
            kicker: 'THE LONG VIEW · EVIDENCE',
            headline: 'The Most Trusted Evidence in Court Spent a Century Without Anybody Checking It',
            standfirst: 'Fingerprints entered the courtroom in 1911 and were treated as infallible for the next ninety years, largely because they looked like science and everyone was busy.',
            byline: 'By the Long View Desk',
            body: [
                'The idea arrived in a scientific journal in 1880, when a Scottish doctor working in Tokyo, Henry Faulds, published a note in Nature suggesting that the ridges on a finger might identify the person who left them at a scene. He had worked it out partly from ancient pottery. Francis Galton put statistics under it in 1892 and estimated the odds of two prints matching by chance as vanishingly small, and Edward Henry’s classification system, developed in colonial Bengal and adopted from 1897, solved the practical problem — not whether prints differ, but how you find one print in a cabinet holding a hundred thousand. Scotland Yard set up its bureau in 1901. The technology of the fingerprint is not the finger. It is the filing system.',
                'The courtroom took it fast. In 1911 the Illinois Supreme Court upheld a murder conviction in People v. Jennings, the first American appellate decision to admit fingerprint identification, and the pattern for the century was set: the evidence was described to juries as a positive identification, singular, with no error rate attached, because none had ever been measured. The FBI opened its identification division in 1924 and the national collection grew into the tens of millions of cards. What was never done, in all that time, was the ordinary scientific step of testing how often trained examiners comparing partial, smudged, real-world marks actually get it wrong.',
                'Two events broke the spell. In 2004, after the Madrid train bombings, the FBI matched a print from the scene to Brandon Mayfield, an Oregon lawyer, with three examiners concurring; he was detained for two weeks, and the Spanish police, who had said from early on that the identification was wrong, eventually matched the print to an Algerian man. The Bureau apologised and later settled. Then in 2009 the National Academy of Sciences published a review of forensic science in the United States and concluded, in careful language that landed like a demolition, that with the exception of nuclear DNA analysis no forensic method had been rigorously shown to be able to consistently and with a high degree of certainty demonstrate a connection between evidence and a specific individual.',
                'What changed after that is smaller than it should be, and more honest than it was. Error rates have finally been measured, and they are low but not zero. Examiners in many laboratories no longer testify to a match to the exclusion of all others in the world; they are instructed to describe a conclusion, and to say that it is theirs. That is the entire reform, and it is worth understanding what kind of reform it is. Nothing about fingers changed in 2009. What changed was the sentence the expert is allowed to say out loud, and it turns out that a sentence was doing most of the work all along.',
            ],
        },
        rail: {
            kicker: 'THE GREEN ROOM · BOOKS',
            headline: 'She Sold a Billion Books by Refusing to Use Any of the Evidence',
            byline: 'By the Green Room Desk',
            body: [
                'Agatha Christie wrote her first novel during the First World War, working in a hospital dispensary, and the job supplied the tool: she knew poisons, in doses, professionally, and she used them with an accuracy that pharmacists wrote to her about for the next fifty years. The Mysterious Affair at Styles was written around 1916 and published in 1920 after several rejections. What made her method durable was a decision she never announced. Her detectives do not do forensics. They do not dust, measure, photograph or type blood. They sit in the room and listen to people arrange their own alibis, and the solution comes out of a contradiction in a sentence somebody said on page forty.',
                'She broke her own genre on purpose in 1926 with The Murder of Roger Ackroyd, a book whose ending caused a public argument about whether the author had cheated — she had not, and the argument is the point, since the reader had been given everything and had declined to suspect the source. Later that same year she vanished. Her car was found abandoned, a national search followed with the newspapers running it as a scandal, and she was located eleven days later at a hotel in Harrogate, registered under another name. She never explained it, not in the autobiography, not once, and the absence of an explanation from the most explanatory writer in England is the most interesting thing she ever wrote.',
                'The long game was the other trick. She wrote Poirot’s final case during the Second World War, locked the manuscript in a bank vault, and left it there for thirty years to be published as Curtain in 1975 — so that when she died the following year, the ending already existed and nobody else could touch it. The Times of New York ran an obituary for the detective on its front page. Her play The Mousetrap opened in London in 1952 and simply kept running, decade after decade. She is the best-selling novelist in the history of the form, and the whole machine is built on the assumption that people give themselves away in conversation, which no laboratory has ever managed to improve on.',
            ],
        },
