            standfirst: 'Two questions went round Tonio Town this week: who decides what o’clock it is, and whether doing the same thing slower counts as doing it better. The night shift answered first, as the night shift usually does.',
            turns: [
                {
                    q: 'You work twelve-hour shifts. Is the time real to you?',
                    a: 'It is the most real thing there is and it is completely made up, and I have made my peace with both. The chart says seven to seven. The body says something else entirely around four in the morning, and the body is not consulted. I will tell you the part that gets me. When the clocks go back, my shift is thirteen hours long, and I am paid for twelve, and nobody in that building thinks this is strange, because a committee said so in a year I was not born in. That is not a complaint. That is just what it is to work inside somebody else’s arithmetic.',
                },
                {
                    q: 'The article says the railways changed the time before any government did.',
                    a: 'That is the whole nineteenth century in one sentence and I am going to use it on Monday. Understand what happened there. It was not a philosophy. Two trains, one track, two honest noons — that is a funeral, and funerals write more law than philosophers do. What I try to get across to seventeen-year-olds is that the big change is never announced as a change. Nobody stood up in 1883 and said, we are abolishing the sun. They said, the timetable will be easier to read.',
                },
                {
                    q: 'And the pianist who played the same piece slower twenty-six years later?',
                    a: 'THAT IS THE ONE I WANT TO TALK ABOUT. I have played the same eight bars for four years. Four years. The notes have not moved. What moves is how much of me is behind them, and last spring, for about nine seconds in a car park, all of it was, and I have been chasing that ever since. So no, I do not think he got slower. I think he stopped being in a hurry to be believed. There is a difference and you can hear it and it took him twenty-six years, which is depressing, because I have four.',
                },
                {
                    q: 'Does any of it change what happens in here?',
                    a: 'Sweetheart, I open at five thirty whatever the government says. But I will give you this. A man came in for thirty-one years and always sat at the counter, and one year he started sitting in the booth, same coffee, same order, same everything, just slower. His wife had died in the March. Nobody asked and nobody moved him. So when your paper tells me a man played the same tune slower at the end, I do not need the record. I have seen the booth.',
                },
            ],
