        voices: {
            kicker: 'VOICES · THE TOWN ANSWERS',
            headline: '“Everything in My Kitchen Comes Off a Truck Now. I Am Not Going to Pretend That Made It Worse.”',
            standfirst: 'Two questions went round Tonio Town this week: what it costs a place when the work leaves without an argument, and whether it is ever sensible to make yourself harder to buy. The diner went first. The diner usually goes first.',
            turns: [
                {
                    q: 'The article says the dockers were not beaten in a negotiation. They were made unnecessary by a specification.',
                    a: 'Then somebody should say the sentence out loud in a hall, because that is the sentence. I have been in the room for the other kind. The other kind you can lose and still know who beat you. This kind, there is nobody to be angry at, so people go home and are angry at the house. And I will tell you what nobody prints. The men were fine for about four years. It was the second four that took them.',
                },
                {
                    q: 'You buy for a kitchen every week. Has the box been good to you?',
                    a: 'Honey, in 1979 I could not get a decent tomato in February at any price, and now I can get one at a price I would have laughed at, and it tastes like a wet card. So both things are true and I refuse to pick. What changed is not the tomato. What changed is that a bad tomato in February is now normal, and a good one in August is now something you have to explain to people under thirty.',
                },
                {
                    q: 'And the singer who deliberately made himself harder to sell?',
                    a: 'Most people who say they did that lost the argument first and told the story afterwards. He did it while they were still writing him cheques, which is the only version that counts. And notice what he actually sued over. Not the songs. The voice. He worked out that the sellable part of him was the part he could not put in a box, and then somebody built a copy of the unboxable part, and he took them to court over it and won. That is not a rock star story. That is a warehouse story.',
                },
                {
                    q: 'Do the two pieces connect?',
                    a: 'This is the energy of the whole century in one page, and I am not being spiritual, I am being literal. One is about making everything the same size so it can travel. The other is about a man who made himself the wrong size on purpose so he could not. And the thing is, both of them got what they wanted. The box got the world. He got to keep his own throat. Nobody in this town gets both, and I would like that read out at the block party.',
                },
            ],
        },
        story: {
            kicker: 'FICTION · TONIO TOWN',
            headline: 'The Wrong Size',
            cast: 'Flo · Reggie · a delivery in a standard case',
            byline: 'A Tonio Times Original',
            body: [
                'The new supplier delivers on Tuesdays in identical grey cases, and every case is the same size, and this is the entire problem.',
                'Flo’s walk-in was built in 1961 for crates. Deep, narrow, and eleven inches shorter than the new grey case, which means the door does not close on a full delivery, which means every Tuesday somebody has to take one case back out and put it on the floor by the pie fridge.',
                '“Call them,” said Reggie, from the counter, where he has been complaining about this establishment daily since the Nixon administration. “Tell them to send a smaller box.”',
                '“There is no smaller box.”',
                '“There is always a smaller box.”',
                '“Reggie, there are two sizes in the world and I get the larger one.”',
                'Reggie considered this. Then he got off the stool, which he does perhaps four times a year, and looked into the walk-in for a long moment.',
                '“Take the shelf out.”',
                '“That shelf is original.”',
                '“So am I,” said Reggie, “and I have been moved twice.”',
                'They took the shelf out. The case went in. The door closed. It took nine minutes and cost nothing, and Flo has been paying a boy an extra half hour every Tuesday for eleven months.',
                'Reggie sat back down and ordered the same thing he has ordered since 1994.',
                '“You could have said that in November,” said Flo.',
                '“You could have asked in November.”',
                '— Flo put the shelf in the alley. It was gone by Thursday. Reggie has not mentioned it, which is how the town knows exactly where it went.',
            ],
        },
