        voices: {
            kicker: 'VOICES · THE TOWN ANSWERS',
            headline: '“I Have Been Asked for a Document Every Tuesday for Six Years. I Have Never Once Had All of It.”',
            standfirst: 'Two questions went round Tonio Town this week: who is allowed to ask you who you are, and whether a person is permitted to become somebody else halfway through. The answers came back in the usual order — the DMV first, the diner second.',
            turns: [
                {
                    q: 'You have opinions about paperwork.',
                    a: 'Not opinions. Experience. There is a list at window six of the things that prove you, and it is longer than the things a man actually has. Two proofs of address, and a bill in your own name, and something with a raised seal on it. I have lived here seventy-one years. I was born nine blocks from that counter. And I am still, on any given Tuesday, one utility bill short of existing. I am not complaining. I am reporting.',
                },
                {
                    q: 'The article argues the change was administrative rather than political.',
                    a: 'Twenty-six seniors. Nine of them have a parent who files something every single year and cannot plan a holiday until it comes back. That is my class, that is not a statistic from a newspaper. And the sentence in that piece that should frighten people is not the one about the wall. It is the one in 2008 about two systems being pointed at each other. Nobody voted for a join. A join is not a policy, it is a Tuesday afternoon of somebody’s work, and it moved more people than the fence did.',
                },
                {
                    q: 'And the singer who changes her sound every record?',
                    a: 'I changed the pie once. Nineteen ninety-eight. Peach to peach and bourbon. I lost four customers and I gained nine and I have heard about the four every year since, including from two of the nine. So she does that on purpose, six times, in front of everybody? Good for her. But somebody in her life is keeping the list of the four. There is always a list of the four. You just do not put it on the record sleeve.',
                },
                {
                    q: 'The two stories do not seem related.',
                    a: 'They are the same story. One is about people who have to keep proving they are who they said they were, on demand, to a stranger with a screen. The other is about a woman who refuses to keep proving it and pays for the refusal in ticket sales. People like a lot of things that are wrong, and the thing they are wrong about here is thinking the second one is a luxury. It is the same tax. She is simply paying it in a nicer room.',
                },
            ],
        },
        story: {
            kicker: 'FICTION · TONIO TOWN',
            headline: 'The Sixth Window',
            cast: 'Vernon · Gloria · a machine that found everything',
            byline: 'A Tonio Times Original',
            body: [
                'They installed the new system on a Monday, which was the first sign. Nothing that arrives on a Monday intends to be temporary.',
                'Vernon was at the door at eight, as he has been at the door at eight for six years, carrying the green folder and, in the green folder, three of the four documents required. The fourth was at home on the kitchen table, under the sugar, where he had left it.',
                'Gloria took the folder at window six and fed it to the machine.',
                '“Mr. Vernon,” she said. “You’re complete.”',
                '“That is not possible.”',
                '“It pulled your address off the county assessor. And your signature off a jury form from March.” She turned the screen an inch, which she is not supposed to do. “According to this, you are entirely established.”',
                'Vernon looked at the green folder, which now had nothing to do, and then at the waiting room, where the television was playing a music special with the sound off and the captions on. A young woman was explaining that she had changed her sound again and expected to lose people.',
                '“She gets new papers every couple of years, that one,” said Vernon.',
                '“That is not what that is.”',
                '“It is exactly what that is.”',
                'Gloria printed something, stamped it twice, and slid it under the glass. Vernon read it once, and then read it again, more slowly.',
                '“This is a real form.”',
                '“It is a real form,” said Gloria. “Annual. Everyone in the county who owns nothing that moves has to file it. Bring it back signed. Tomorrow is fine.”',
                '— It is a real form. Gloria has processed it eleven times in nine years. Nine of them were his, and she has never once mentioned that the machine could have signed it for him.',
            ],
        },
