            headline: 'The Word “Captured” Escaped From Economics, and Now Nobody Can Answer It',
            standfirst: 'Yesterday’s clip is an argument about immigration. This desk has no vote in it. It does have a professional interest in the one adjective that ends every conversation it enters.',
            byline: 'By the Listening Desk',
            body: [
                'The phrase is thrown away near the end, the way the good ones always are: it’s just they’re ideologically captured. Captured has a real address. In 1971 the economist George Stigler published a paper arguing that regulators tend, over time, to be taken over by the industries they regulate — not bribed, simply surrounded, until the industry’s way of seeing becomes the only available way of seeing. He called it capture, and it won him a great deal of professional respect and, eventually, a Nobel Prize. It is a serious idea about institutions.',
                'What happened next is a lesson in how English works. The word escaped the discipline, dropped the institution, and attached itself to individuals — so that captured now means, in ordinary online argument, this person can no longer see. Note the elegance of the accusation and then note the problem with it. Capture is unfalsifiable by design. If the accused disagrees, that is the capture talking. It is the same structure as calling somebody hysterical, and it is worth knowing precisely because you will meet it, and because the correct answer is not to protest that you are not captured. There isn’t one. Ask instead what evidence would change the speaker’s own mind, which is the only question the frame has no answer to.',
                'The rest of the clip is standard political English and all of it is useful. It suddenly became a calling card for the party — a calling card was the engraved visiting card left on a tray in a nineteenth-century hall, so a calling card is the thing that announces you before you are in the room. President Trump ran on this issue — to run for office is American, from the race, and the preposition matters: you run on an issue and against a person. And there’s probably a lot of people that got duped into coming to this country — dupe is from French, often traced to a bird thought to be gullible, and note the construction, duped into doing, which is the standard English frame for a deception that produced an action.',
                'One more, and it is the most quietly loaded phrase in the piece: this is the first time in history that the border has been wide open. Wide open takes an adjective that describes a door and applies it to a line on a map, and once that image is accepted the argument is largely over, because doors have owners and a duty to be shut. This is the ordinary work of political language in every country, and it is done with prepositions and adjectives rather than with facts. The rule to carry away: when a conversation reaches a word you cannot argue with — captured, wide open, so-called — stop arguing with the word. Ask what would count as evidence. Then listen for whether the answer describes anything that could actually happen.',
            ],
