            headline: '“Everybody Says Trust Was Lost. Some of Us Were Never Issued Any.”',
            standfirst: 'Our leader examines the language people use when they have stopped believing an institution. Aisha, of Tonio Town, who was called for jury service and told the room she did not think the system worked, would like to be more precise than she was allowed to be that day.',
            turns: [
                { q: 'You said in open court that you were sceptical.', a: 'I said I did not believe it worked the same for everybody, which is a different sentence, and they wrote down the shorter one. That is the whole of my complaint about institutions in one afternoon. I gave them a careful thing and the form had a smaller box. Nobody was dishonest. The box was just smaller than what I said.' },
                { q: 'Our leader would say that is a reason to argue, not to withdraw.', a: 'Your leader has a byline. When I argue, I am difficult. When he argues, he is thorough. I have thought about this a great deal, and I have decided the answer is not to stop arguing, it is to stop arguing about the verdict and start arguing about the box. Whoever sizes the box has already decided most of it, and nobody is ever in the room when that happens.' },
                { q: 'And yet you served.', a: 'Eleven days. And I will tell you what changed and what did not. What did not change: I still think it works better for some people. What changed: I sat with eleven strangers who had all arrived certain and who all, at some point over those eleven days, said out loud that they had been wrong about something. Every single one of them. I have never seen that anywhere else in my life, and I include churches, and I include my own family.' },
            ],
