            headline: '“Everybody Wants to Prosecute. Nobody Wants to Stand at the Door.”',
            standfirst: 'Our leader admires a politician who calls disagreement an office. Bailiff Reyes, who has kept order in the Wexford County courtroom for eleven years and is the brother of an English teacher at Lincoln High, does the part of it nobody applauds.',
            turns: [
                { q: 'You have watched more arguments than anybody in this county.', a: 'I have watched more people wait to argue. That is the job I actually do. The lawyers get eleven minutes each and they have rehearsed them. Everybody else in that room has been holding one sentence since March, and my whole function is to make sure they do not say it. It is not that the sentence is wrong. Half the time it is the truest thing anybody would say all day. It is that if it gets said, we start again in October.' },
                { q: 'Our leader would say opposition is a duty.', a: 'It is, and it is paid, and it has a chair. That is the part people skip. Disagreeing is not brave in that building — it is scheduled. What is difficult is the man in the third row who disagrees with everybody and has no chair and no eleven minutes, and who is right, and who will be removed by me if he stands up. I have removed him. I did not enjoy it. I would do it again on Monday.' },
                { q: 'Does the job get easier?', a: 'It got quieter. The first year I thought my job was silence. Now I think my job is order, and those are not the same thing at all — a room can be completely silent and about to come apart, and you learn to hear it. My sister teaches teenagers. She says the same thing about a classroom, and neither of us can explain to anybody how we know.' },
            ],
