        voices: {
            kicker: 'VOICES · THE COUNTERPOINT',
            headline: '“Everybody Romanticises a Place With No Rules. I Work the Front Desk.”',
            standfirst: 'Our leader reports admiringly from a region the law does not reach. Patrice, who has run the front counter at the Tonio Town branch of the Department of Motor Vehicles for nineteen years, would like to speak up for the queue.',
            turns: [
                { q: 'You are the most complained-about woman in this county.', a: 'I am the most complained-to. There is a difference and I have had nineteen years to work out exactly where it sits. Nobody has ever complained about me. They complain at me, about a rule I did not write, in a building I did not design, on a Tuesday I did not choose. And every one of them says the same sentence on the way out: this place is a joke. Then they come back in ninety days and I renew them.' },
                { q: 'Our leader is impressed by a place where none of this exists.', a: 'Then your leader should go and stand in one. I have had two customers in nineteen years who had actually lived somewhere the paperwork did not work, and they are the only two who have ever said thank you at the window. Not polite. Grateful. You can hear the difference. Everybody else has never once had to picture the alternative, which is a luxury so complete that it is invisible to the person holding it.' },
                { q: 'People say the wait is the problem.', a: 'The wait is the product. That is what I would put on a sign if they let me. You are not waiting for a licence. You are waiting for everybody ahead of you to be treated exactly the way you are about to be treated, and there is no faster way to do that. Anybody who tells you there is, is selling you a place nearer the front, and somebody standing in that line is paying for it.' },
            ],
        },
