            headline: 'Running Dry Runs of Problems',
            byline: 'By the Green Room Desk',
            body: [
                'Jesse Eisenberg — Oscar-nominated for The Social Network, and again for writing A Real Pain, which he also directed — eats hot wings against medical advice and gives away the best psychological definition of the week. Asked about anxiety, he describes it as running through dry runs of problems. A dry run is a rehearsal without the live component: without water in the hoses, without ammunition in the guns. He is saying anxiety is a rehearsal for events that will not happen, staged at full production cost. Nobody in the therapy aisle has ever put it better in six words.',
                'The host supplies the other great image: it is step by step, we are boiling the frog one degree at a time. The fable — that a frog will sit still while the water heats slowly — is not actually true of frogs, which leave, but it has survived because it describes something real about people, who do not. Then, of Eisenberg himself: you are bucking any sort of trend or preconception one could have about you, buck being what a horse does to remove a rider, and the standard English verb for refusing a direction everyone else is going in.',
                'The line to take home is the most honest one. Of a discomfort he has carried since his first job: all these years in, I can never shake that. Shake it, as a dog shakes water off, is what English says about a feeling you have tried to remove and could not. Not overcome, not process — shake. It admits that the method was physical and desperate and did not work. He also offers we are battle tested and, of an interviewer who goes deeper than most, you delve into the actor’s process. Delve is an old digging word — a delver worked with a spade — and it is the right verb for the difference between asking a question and going down after the answer.',
            ],
