        lead: {
            kicker: 'THE BIG LISTEN · JRE CLIPS',
            headline: 'Ride That Thing for as Much Juice as You Can Get',
            standfirst: 'Yesterday’s clip is a comedian explaining a controversy from the inside — and, without meaning to, describing the business model of everybody who was angry at him.',
            byline: 'By the Listening Desk',
            body: [
                'First, what a roast is, because the form explains the argument. The New York Friars Club had been holding them since the middle of the last century, and the Dean Martin Celebrity Roasts put the shape on television in the 1970s: the guest of honour sits at the front while their friends say the worst things they can invent, and then the guest gets the last word. The contract is that everything said is a compliment wearing a disguise, and the proof is that you are there at all. Nobody roasts a stranger. Tony Hinchcliffe, who hosts Kill Tony and has written for and performed at these things for years, is operating inside a form with rules older than he is.',
                'His technical account of the night is the most interesting part. Instead of milking it and getting applause breaks, he says, he decided to blast off more jokes per minute than I normally do. Milk it is dairy — extracting the last of something — and in comedy it means holding a laugh past its natural length. What he is describing is a deliberate density strategy: refuse the applause, keep firing. That is craft talk, and it is worth noting that the craft decision is the one thing nobody argued about afterwards.',
                'Then he describes what happens next, and this is the sentence to write down: their business is people are mad at them, they have hot takes. They were trying to bait everybody. And of a piece of material once it has caught: you just ride that thing for as much juice as you can get out of it and then you move on to the next thing. Read those two together. He is describing the outrage industry and his own industry in identical language — bait, juice, ride it, move on. He does not seem to notice. That absence of noticing is the most honest moment in the tape.',
                'A note on nitpick, which he uses of the people going through the joke line by line. It is literal: picking the eggs of lice out of hair, one at a time, close work with no reward. English chose it for pedantic criticism because the image contains both the tininess and the tedium. And finally, for the joy of it, then it is on like Donkey Kong — a rhyming intensifier from the 1981 arcade game, meaning the thing has properly started. English will accept absolutely anything as a phrase provided it rhymes. This is not a weakness of the language. It is most of its history.',
            ],
        },
        rail: {
            kicker: 'THE GREEN ROOM · MUSIC',
            headline: 'Your Flowers, While You Can Still Smell Them',
            byline: 'By the Green Room Desk',
            body: [
                'Eric Gales plays a right-handed guitar flipped upside down and strung as he found it, because that is how he taught himself as a child, and he made his first record at sixteen. He has been open about the years he lost and the years he has taken back. Rick Beato opens by telling him: I want to make sure you get your flowers while you can smell them. The full proverb — give people their flowers while they can still smell them — is a plain instruction about funerals, which is where flowers arrive too late to be any use to the person they are for. There is no more useful sentence in English to have ready for somebody you admire.',
                'His advice to young players is the sharpest thing in the interview: it is great to want to emulate these people, but at a certain point you have to cement your own DNA. Cement as a verb, applied to something biological — that is a mixed metaphor and it is better than a clean one, because it contains both the setting and the permanence. Beside it, on gear he has not explored: I have not had an opportunity to go down the rabbit hole in. And on not wanting a sponsorship: I do not want to be under the thumb of getting text messages and emails from the company. Under the thumb is pressure applied by a hand that is not doing anything else.',
                'The business paragraph should be read by every musician in Japan. Streaming is pennies on the dollar. A good opening slot was great if you were on tour with somebody who was putting butts in seats. And if you do not have this viral thing about you, then you have got to go boots on the ground, man — the military phrase, borrowed for the work of driving to a town and playing in it. His timing phrase is the one to steal: at the drop of a dime, I am going to make a change. It is a crossbreed of at the drop of a hat and dropping a dime in a payphone, and it means instantly, without notice. Nobody planned it. English made it anyway.',
            ],
        },
