            headline: 'From Whole Cloth',
            standfirst: 'Yesterday’s clip is two men who have made films for thirty years arguing that the machine will be slower than everybody thinks. The key phrase in their argument once meant its own opposite.',
            byline: 'By the Listening Desk',
            body: [
                'Ben Affleck and Matt Damon wrote Good Will Hunting together and won the screenplay Oscar for it in 1998, and they now run a production company together, so when they discuss what a machine can and cannot do to a film they are talking about their own trade. Affleck’s claim is narrow and specific: the fear that it is going to be making movies from whole cloth — that runs counter, in my view, to what history seems to show, which is that adoption is slow. Note the modesty of the position. He is not saying it cannot. He is saying the calendar is wrong, and calendars are the thing forecasters get wrong most.',
                'From whole cloth deserves its own paragraph, because it is one of the few English phrases that has completely reversed. Originally, cloth of the full width straight from the loom was the genuine article, and made of whole cloth meant honest and unpatched. By the nineteenth century, in America, it had flipped to mean entirely fabricated, invented out of nothing. The same four words now say the opposite of what they said. If you ever wonder whether English can be trusted to keep its meanings, here is your answer, and it is the phrase people reach for to talk about things that are not real.',
                'The rest of the vocabulary is about a curve and a mood. The early AI, the line went up very steeply, and it is now sort of levelling off — level off is what an aircraft does when it stops climbing, and it is the most common English shape for arguing that a trend has spent itself. Against it sits this sense, this existential dread, and the flat prediction it is going to wipe everything out. Dread is not fear. Fear has an object. Dread is the one that does not, which is precisely why it is the right word for an argument about a technology nobody can date.',
                'Two more to keep. His friend finally kind of holds his feet to the fire — a medieval torture that survives as the standard phrase for making somebody answer properly. And, said of a performance: you see him and he is kind of tap dancing, meaning improvising to fill time and cover a gap. If you learn only one of today’s items, take that one. Tap dancing is what English calls the specific, visible, energetic activity of a person who does not have the answer, and once you have the phrase you will see it in every meeting you attend for the rest of your life.',
            ],
