        lead: {
            kicker: 'THE BIG LISTEN · JRE CLIPS',
            headline: 'The Experts Hated the Hit',
            standfirst: 'Yesterday’s clip contains one of the great professional humiliations of popular music, told cheerfully by the man it happened to. The English of being told no is worth the whole tape.',
            byline: 'By the Listening Desk',
            body: [
                'The speaker is John Mellencamp, born in Seymour, Indiana in 1951, who spent the first years of his career released under a stage name he had not chosen and did not want — his early manager put out the records as Johnny Cougar. The song under discussion is Jack & Diane, from the 1982 album American Fool, which went to number one in the United States and is the only single of his that ever did. The people paid to know things did not want it. That is the whole story, and it is older than he is.',
                'Watch the register of the refusal. And if you do not get on with it, we are going to drop you from the label — get on with it is the impatient English imperative for stop preparing and start, and drop is what a label does when it stops answering. Then his own summary of the lesson he took: so, keep it simple, stupid. That is not folk wisdom, it is engineering. The KISS principle came out of American aircraft design around 1960, from Kelly Johnson at Lockheed’s Skunk Works, and it means a system should be repairable in a field by an ordinary mechanic. The song that survived is the one built like that.',
                'The best phrase in the clip is grew up in public. I was growing still, because I grew up in public. English has no single noun for this — the condition of doing your unfinished years in front of strangers — and so it builds one from the two plainest words available. It applies to musicians, to child actors, to anybody who was promoted early. Note the tense: grew, finished, and the growing that was still happening, unfinished. He puts both in one sentence without apologising for the contradiction.',
                'Two more that carry their history. If you give them an inch, they will find a reason not to — the proverb is give them an inch and they will take a mile, and the truncated version is now more common than the full one, which is worth knowing when you hear only the first half. And how could you be more wrong than Jack and Diane? — a rhetorical question used not to ask but to hold a grudge in place. Forty-four years is a long time to keep a receipt. He has kept it, and he is laughing, and both of those are true at once.',
            ],
        },
        rail: {
            kicker: 'THE GREEN ROOM · SCREEN & STAGE',
            headline: 'A Good Tennis Partner',
            byline: 'By the Green Room Desk',
            body: [
                'Claire Danes and Richard Gadd talk shop, and it is the sort of conversation where two people who do the same difficult job stop explaining and start comparing. Danes has been famous since My So-Called Life in 1994 and carried Homeland for eight years; Gadd wrote and performed Baby Reindeer, which began as a one-man show in Edinburgh and became, on Netflix in 2024, the most argued-about thing on television. So when he says this was no IP, this is raw material, he means it literally: no book, no franchise, no rights deal — his own life, unprocessed.',
                'The phrase to take home is we contain multitudes, which is Walt Whitman from 1855: I am large, I contain multitudes. Whitman wrote it as a reply to being accused of contradicting himself, which is exactly how Gadd uses it. That is the mark of a quotation worth learning — it still does its original job. Beside it, Danes on long-running characters: you have to find new things within them, and it is kind of a blessing and a curse. And on the risky scripts she resents and then loves: high risk, high reward. I resent them initially, and then I am so grateful for them.',
                'The most quietly brilliant image is hers. Asked how she held on to a character across years, she says the image that always came to mind was putting vodka in the freezer. I was like, she will be there. Nothing announces itself. There is no metaphor about acting anywhere in that sentence, only a bottle that does not freeze and does not go anywhere, waiting in the cold for whenever you next want it. And to describe a scene partner she uses a good tennis partner — someone whose return is worth playing. Steal that one for colleagues. It is the kindest professional compliment in English.',
            ],
        },
