            body: [
                'Penélope Cruz — an Oscar for Vicky Cristina Barcelona, a long collaboration with Pedro Almodóvar that runs from Volver to Parallel Mothers — eats the wings and gives away the most useful professional secret of the week. Asked about saying that she feels completely lost at the start of every project, like a fish out of water, and hopes she never loses the feeling, she does not soften it. The phrase itself is very old, in English since Chaucer’s century, and it has survived because the picture is instant: not a bad fish, a fish in the wrong medium.',
                'The best line is about her therapist: she knows all the theory, and she knows how to press your buttons and how to ask you the right questions so that you can figure it out. Press your buttons is a compliment here, which is unusual — normally it means to provoke somebody deliberately, and the image is a control panel that somebody else has learned. Kept beside it is the ultimate push to do it, said of the husband who bought a car for a woman afraid of driving. Push, not encouragement. English prefers physical nouns for motivation.',
                'And then the host, closing the show, produces two phrases in one breath: here you are conquering the wings of death and living to tell the tale, so there is nothing left to do but roll out the red carpet for you. Living to tell the tale is the survivor’s formula. Rolling out the red carpet is literal history — the phrase spread from a train, the Twentieth Century Limited, which in 1902 ran a crimson carpet along the platform to guide passengers aboard. That is where the whole language of glamour comes from. A carpet. On a platform. To stop people getting lost.',
            ],
