            headline: 'A Seed, a Circle, and a Word for Sincerity That Embarrasses You',
            standfirst: 'Yesterday’s clip is an actor explaining his craft without any of the usual fog. The interesting English is in how he describes a life shaped by one early moment.',
            byline: 'By the Listening Desk',
            body: [
                'Bradley Cooper is talking about American Sniper, the 2014 film directed by Clint Eastwood in which he played the marksman Chris Kyle, and for which he was nominated for the Academy Award for best actor. He produced it as well. If you want the range of the man in three titles, put beside it Silver Linings Playbook, A Star Is Born — which he directed, co-wrote and starred in — and Maestro, in which he directed himself as Leonard Bernstein. The clip is about preparation, and it is unusually free of mystique.',
                'Two metaphors carry the whole conversation and they are worth separating. That planted a seed that changed your whole life — agricultural, slow, and crucially something done to you by somebody else. And I have come full circle — geometric, complete, and something you notice only from the end. English hands you these two shapes for a life and they are not interchangeable. A seed explains a beginning you did not choose. A circle explains a present you did not expect. Most people’s stories need both, and most people only own one.',
                'Then the technical joy: I have to factor in the curve of the earth. He is describing what he learned in conjunction with learning about sniping, and the sentence is a gift, because factor in is the most useful business verb in English — to include a variable in a calculation — and here it is being used about the planet. Note also everybody defers to the boss, where defer to means to yield judgement to somebody with more authority, and it is the polite word for a hierarchy nobody wants to name.',
                'The word to take away is hokey. It sounds so hokey, you know, I get it. It comes from hokum, early-twentieth-century American theatre slang for cheap emotional material that reliably works on an audience. Hokey is what English calls sincerity that has embarrassed itself — not false, just too obviously aiming at feeling. And notice what he does with it: he says it about his own explanation, before anybody else can, and then continues anyway. That is the professional move. Name the cringe, then say the true thing regardless.',
            ],
