                'Sean Ono Lennon, born in 1975 on his father’s birthday, sits with Rick Beato — the guitarist and teacher whose long interviews have become the most serious music education on the internet — and offers the sharpest industrial metaphor of the month. Honestly, I think in a way the music industry was a guinea pig, or a canary in the coal mine. The second image is literal history: from 1911 British coal mines carried canaries underground because the bird collapses from carbon monoxide long before a man notices it, and the practice was only retired in 1986. His point is that music was digitised first, and everything that then happened to musicians is now happening to everybody else.',
                'The self-correction is the best part of his English. He says the moral of the story is that I actually regret that influence, and then explains why: what separates the music I like from the music I dislike is more of a taste thing. More of a X thing is a construction worth stealing whole — it downgrades a claim from law to preference in four words, and English speakers use it constantly to avoid sounding like they are legislating. Similarly careful: I do not like to put people down, but I think this is a safe one to say.',
                'The line to write on your wall is on paper. The amount of inspiration and education you can get from listening to anything you want — on paper, that sounds like it is going to make a generation of the best musicians of all time. On paper means in theory, before contact with reality, and it is the most economical way in English to introduce a disappointment. He also says musicians have been taken down many notches, from the notches cut in a belt or a gunstock to measure rank. When a language wants to describe a fall in status, it almost always reaches for something you can count.',
