            headline: 'The Six Words That Do All the Work',
            standfirst: 'Yesterday’s clip contains a confession — and the confession has a noun in it that quietly moves the crime. Learn to hear that noun and half of public English opens up.',
            byline: 'By the Listening Desk',
            body: [
                'The sentence is: we absolutely screwed up the comms of the Epstein files. Read it twice. The verb is honest, the adverb is generous, and then comes comms — the communications, the rollout, the messaging. What is being confessed is not the handling of the documents but the handling of the announcement. English has a whole family of these nouns (the optics, the messaging, the rollout, the framing) and each one performs the same trick: it accepts blame while relocating it one step away from the thing itself. This is not a lie. It is a smaller truth, offered first, in the hope that it satisfies.',
                'Around that sentence sits the vocabulary of a man defending a position while conceding ground. She got roasted for it publicly by a lot of people including me — roast is a cookery word that in American English became public ridicule, and the passive got roasted lets the punishment happen without naming who swung. I have probably gone down every single rabbit hole we could go down: from Lewis Carroll in 1865, now the standard English metaphor for research that eats a night. And on the up and up, an Americanism from the 1860s meaning above board, honest — the sort of phrase that sounds folksy and is doing legal work.',
                'The strongest item is flooding the zone. It is a defensive formation in American football — put more bodies in an area than the offence can throw into — and it crossed into politics as a media strategy: release so much at once that nothing can be read. The clip uses it in a genuinely interesting way, as a method of discrediting a conspiracy theory rather than a way to hide. Note the shape of that argument. If you bury a true thing inside a hundred absurd things, the true thing becomes absurd by association. That is a claim about attention, not about facts, and it is the most modern sentence in the tape.',
                'Then there is original sin, which the speaker uses to mean the first error from which every later error descends. It is a theological term, and English keeps reaching for it because we have no secular word for a mistake that is inherited. Watch what the phrase does: it makes the fault old, structural, nobody’s fault in particular. Between the comms and the original sin, this clip is a masterclass in the two directions English lets you push responsibility — sideways into the process, or backwards into history. Learn to notice both. You will never listen to a press conference the same way.',
            ],
