                'Colman Domingo and Sarah Pidgeon spend an hour trading shop, and the shop talk is where the English is. Domingo — Oscar-nominated for Rustin in 2023 and again for Sing Sing, and known to a much larger audience as Ali in Euphoria — describes the moment a scene finally lands: by the time we get to the third take, I am in the money now. We are grooving now. In the money comes from horse racing, where the first three finishers are paid. Right in my pocket, which he uses of lines he has not yet mastered, comes from music: in the pocket is what a rhythm section is when the groove locks. Both mean arrived, and neither mentions arriving.',
                'Pidgeon, who came up through The Wilds and then Stereophonic on Broadway, offers the line worth stealing: it was so refreshing to play a woman who felt comfortable taking up space. Take up space has travelled a long way in ten years — from a literal complaint to a compliment about presence — and it now does a job no single verb in English can do. Beside it she puts under a microscope for a life examined by strangers, and a drop in the bucket for the fraction of a real person she hopes to carry.',
                'The phrase that ought to end up in your notebook is radical empathy, which Domingo applies to acting class itself: an acting class, it is just radical empathy. He is not being soft. He means the professional obligation to build, from the inside, a person whose choices you would never make. Then the funniest and most dangerous line in the conversation — I try to make big swings immediately in every direction — a baseball metaphor that admits the strikeouts in advance. That is how professionals talk about risk. They name the swing, not the hit.',
