        lead: {
            kicker: 'THE BIG LISTEN · JRE CLIPS',
            headline: 'The Sound of a Man Building Something Out Loud',
            standfirst: 'Yesterday’s clip is not about zombies. It is a recording of an idea assembling itself in real time — and English keeps a special set of verbs for exactly that.',
            byline: 'By the Listening Desk',
            body: [
                'The speaker is Jimmy Donaldson, from Greenville, North Carolina, who under the name MrBeast runs the largest channel on YouTube by making challenges expensive enough to be news. In the clip he is not pitching. He is thinking with his mouth open, and the first thing he does is kill his own idea before anybody else can: you use this foam thing and it is lame, which is why we never got around to it. Get around to it is the phrase English uses for a task that was never refused and never begun. It is the honest verb for a shelf.',
                'Then the idea starts moving, and every verb that carries it is physical. It has got my brain churning — churn is what a butter barrel does, and the word survives in English almost entirely for stomachs and minds. Where my head goes — not what I think, but where the head travels, as though thought were a route. And they are creeping in, of zombies and, in ordinary use, of doubt, of tiredness, of costs. Watch these and you will notice that English almost never describes thinking as thinking. It describes it as motion, weather, or machinery.',
                'The sentence that gives the game away is this one: where my head goes, because the videos are so big, this is probably a five, ten million dollar project. The budget arrives inside the same breath as the idea. That is not a boast, it is a description of the industry he built: at his scale, an idea and an invoice are grammatically the same object, and he cannot imagine the first without pricing the second. Notice too the tiny confidence marker at the end — I think we are on to something now. On to something is what English says at the exact moment an idea stops being a joke.',
                'His reference point is an I Am Legend type deal, which is worth the detour. Richard Matheson published the novel in 1954 and it is the book that quietly invented the modern apocalypse — one survivor, an emptied city, monsters who used to be neighbours — before the 2007 film put Will Smith in a silent Manhattan. Every abandoned-town challenge, every survival format, every game that hands you a bat and an empty street is downstream of that one paperback. When somebody says a type deal in English, they are naming their source without meaning to. Follow it.',
            ],
        },
        rail: {
            kicker: 'THE GREEN ROOM · SCREEN & STAGE',
            headline: 'Put Me On Game',
            byline: 'By the Green Room Desk',
            body: [
                'Millie Bobby Brown eats ten increasingly hostile chicken wings and, between them, closes the book on a decade as Eleven — the character she began playing as a child in Stranger Things and has now finished. Closed the book on is not a fancy way of saying finished. It carries an object you can shut, and a decision to stop reading it, and that is precisely the tone she wants. Filed beside it: being a child actor helps, because I just grew up doing it, so it is very second nature. Second nature — a habit so old it behaves like instinct — is a phrase worth having ready when somebody asks how you do a thing you no longer think about.',
                'The line to steal is put me on game. If you will humour me, I am hoping that you can put me on game for some British classics. It comes out of African-American English, where to put somebody on game is to teach them what everyone else already knows and nobody explains — the unwritten part. There is no clean equivalent in textbook English. Teach me is too school. Fill me in is too small. Put me on game admits that the gap is not information, it is initiation.',
                'The rest is the vocabulary of survival under pressure. She is trying to pace myself, which is a runner’s phrase and the correct one for a table of escalating hot sauce. She says Tom Holland grabbed number ten right out of the gate — a horse-racing image for doing something the instant the race begins. And of somebody who has done this before: that is somebody who has gone through the gauntlet, from the old military punishment of running between two rows of men with sticks. A talk show, a hot wing, a Monday. English keeps reaching for the same three sports: racing, running, and being hit.',
            ],
        },
