            headline: '“I Am Not Angry. I Remember. Those Are Different Buildings.”',
            standfirst: 'Our leader holds up a father who walked into the neighbourhood that killed his son. Dave Boyd, of Tonio Town, has not walked past a brisket since 2019 and would like to make a distinction.',
            turns: [
                { q: 'A man forgave his son’s killer in open court. You have not forgiven a piece of meat.', a: 'That is the joke everybody makes and I will take it, because he is right and I am small. But hear the distinction. Forgiving is something you do to a person. I do not have a person. I have a thermometer, a reading of two hundred and four, and a lid I opened when I should not have. You cannot forgive an appliance. You can only stop reading the note.' },
                { q: 'Your wife has told you for years that everybody has forgotten it.', a: 'I am sure they have. That is not the thing. My boy was fifteen, standing with his friends, and when the plate came out he looked at the grass. What I am carrying is not the brisket, and this town keeps handing me the brisket because it is the lighter of the two objects.' },
                { q: 'The man in your leader went back into the neighbourhood. Is there anywhere you will not go?', a: 'The county fair, every August. And I could go — Tammy would come, nothing would happen. So he is a better man than me and I would like that printed. He walked toward the people who took the thing that cannot be replaced. I will not walk past a tent over a thing that can be cooked again on any Saturday of my life.' },
            ],
