        lead: {
            kicker: 'THE BIG LISTEN · JRE CLIPS',
            headline: 'A Tsunami of Wealth, and Nothing to Do on Tuesday',
            standfirst: 'The philosopher who spent a decade explaining how artificial intelligence might end us now spends it on the harder question: what if it does not, and solves everything instead? Yesterday’s clip is the polite version of a very rude idea about your job.',
            byline: 'By the Listening Desk',
            body: [
                'Nick Bostrom is the Oxford philosopher who wrote Superintelligence in 2014 and made the risk argument respectable enough that governments borrowed his vocabulary; he is also behind the simulation argument, the 2003 paper that keeps resurfacing in bars. His Oxford institute closed in 2024. The book behind this conversation is the other one — Deep Utopia, subtitled Life and Meaning in a Solved World — and its premise is why the clip deserves a second pass. He is not here to frighten you. He is asking what is left of a human being once every problem has been taken care of.',
                'Start with the sentence everyone will repeat: a tsunami of wealth just kind of flows through and lifts all boats. That is a hundred-year-old political slogan — a rising tide lifts all boats — which John F. Kennedy used so often that people assume he wrote it, and which his speechwriter later admitted was picked up from a regional business campaign. It is a beautiful phrase with a hole in it: a tide lifts boats, and it does not lift swimmers. Bostrom does not pretend otherwise. He goes straight to what the slogan is smuggling — and then the idea of human work becomes an anachronism. Anachronism is the strongest word in the clip. Not obsolete, not unnecessary. Out of its time. A wristwatch in a Roman film.',
                'His structure is worth copying whether or not you care about machines. Kind of layers to the onion, he says, and then peels: the most superficial level is, well, they automate your job. Underneath that, they automate the things you did instead of your job. Underneath that is the layer nobody has a plan for, where effort itself stops being how a person earns the right to their own life. He tests it with a deliberate provocation — wage labour is a sort of slavery light, in a sense; you sell a third of your waking day just to pay for necessities. Light, in the diet-cola sense, applied to slavery. It is built to make you flinch and then argue. It works.',
                'Then the escape hatch, which is the most English thing in the conversation. Rather than prescribe a good life, he shrugs: different strokes for different folks. Some will hop on in to whatever virtual world beats this one; some end up in beach chairs sipping drinks all day, and that’s also a kind of checking out; some, he concedes, could end up locked out of regular reality altogether. Underneath it sits the only honest position available: well, the truth is we don’t know, right — how it will pan out. Learn that construction. It is not a failure to have an opinion. It is the correct opinion, in the register of a man who has spent twenty years being wrong carefully.',
            ],
        },
