                'Bell’s compliment to him is the one to keep. You could throw something away, come back, make a little side comment, keep going — effortlessly. To throw a line away is acting jargon: deliver it with deliberately no weight. It exists because of a paradox every performer learns late — the harder you push a funny line, the less funny it gets. Her own confession is the same paradox from below: I didn’t have any funny bullets in my gun that first season.',
                'Best of all is the verb. Describing how she built a character out of a real woman, she says: I just studied her like single white female. That is the title of a 1992 thriller about a woman who copies her flatmate to a frightening degree, used here as a verb with no explanation, because none is expected. Turning a proper noun into a verb is the most native move in English and the hardest to fake — but the mechanism copies. Pick a title everybody in the room knows, and use it as a thing you did.',
