                'He lost. The Supreme Court ruled against him in 1972, five votes to three, on the extraordinary grounds that baseball’s antitrust exemption was an established error that Congress, not the court, ought to fix. Flood never recovered as a player — thirteen games for Washington in 1971, then nothing. He had turned down roughly a hundred thousand dollars, a large salary for the day, and received in exchange a defeat, a ruined career and a long period of drinking in Europe.',
                'Three years after the ruling, an arbitrator struck down the reserve clause in the Messersmith–McNally case, and free agency arrived; average salaries rose from about forty-five thousand dollars in 1975 to nearly two million by the end of the century. Flood earned none of it. This is the shape of an unusual number of important careers: the person who moves the wall is standing where it falls. When players today are asked what they learned from Curt Flood, the honest answer is that they learned nothing from him, because he was not there to be asked. They inherited it.',
