                'In 2001 the Wall Street Journal published an investigation, with players on the record, describing what had been in place at the Polo Grounds since late July: a telescope in the clubhouse beyond centre field, a coach watching the catcher’s signs, and a buzzer wired to the bullpen so the hitter could be told what was coming. Several Giants confirmed it. Thomson, asked directly for the rest of his life, gave answers that moved: sometimes he had not taken the sign on that pitch, sometimes he could not recall, once that he would rather not have known.',
                'What makes the story permanently interesting is that nothing about it is settled by the facts. Stealing signs from the field was, and is, part of the game; a telescope and an electrician is a different animal, and everyone involved knew the difference. Yet Branca still threw the pitch, Thomson still hit it four hundred feet, and a man told what is coming still has to hit a major-league fastball. Baseball has spent seventy-five years unable to decide whether the greatest moment in its history has an asterisk, which may be the most honest position available.',
