                'The first Monday of August had a poor field. Mrs Aoki, who is seventy and runs a dry cleaner’s, had lost a customer of twenty-two years to a nursing home in another prefecture — respectable, but she had known it was coming since spring, and the table docks you for expected losses. A man called Sasaki had lost forty thousand yen on a bet and looked around the table for sympathy, and got the silence that money always gets there. “Money comes back,” said Mrs Aoki. “It does not come back to me,” said Sasaki. “That,” she said, “is a different complaint.”',
                'Ito, who has been coming longest and is a builder with two bad shoulders, said he had lost an argument with his daughter about whether he should still be on roofs. The table leaned in. “Which way did you lose it?” asked Mrs Aoki, because that is the real question. “She proved it,” said Ito. “She had photographs of me on the ladder. She had timed how long it took me to get down.” He turned his glass around. “I have not been up since. I did not agree to that. It just happened, the way a thing does when you know somebody is right.”',
