            headline: 'In Defence of Starting Late',
            standfirst: 'The ten-thousand-hour story says the winners began at four. The wider evidence says something kinder: outside a few narrow games, the ones who wander first tend to arrive further.',
            byline: 'By the Ideas Desk',
            body: [
                'The famous version of excellence has a child at a keyboard aged three and a stopwatch running ever since. It is a real path, and in a narrow class of fields it is close to the only one. Chess, gymnastics, classical violin — these are what psychologists call kind learning environments: the rules never move, the same patterns repeat, feedback is instant and honest. Start early, drill hard, and the hours convert cleanly into skill.',
                'Almost nothing else in life is like that. Most of what we do — business, medicine, writing, teaching, raising anyone — takes place in wicked environments, where the rules shift, the feedback lies or arrives years late, and last season’s winning pattern is this season’s trap. In those fields the research points the other way. The people who do best have usually taken what is called a sampling period: several years of trying things, quitting them, and carrying the leftovers into whatever they finally chose. Roger Federer, whose mother declined to coach him, played squash, badminton, football and basketball long after his rivals had specialised.',
                'The reason is that range is not lost time; it is imported equipment. The doctor who studied literature hears a patient differently. The engineer who once cooked professionally knows what a system under load actually feels like. Nobody starting over at forty is behind the four-year-old — they are playing a different game, one that rewards the width of what you have seen. And on the last day of a month, or a decade, that is the more useful fact: the hours you spent elsewhere were not the detour. Very often they are the only reason you will be good at this.',
            ],
