            standfirst: 'Russian speakers see two blues where English sees one. An Australian language has no word for left — and its speakers never get lost. The tongue you think in is quietly editing the world.',
            byline: 'By the Language Desk',
            body: [
                'Russian has no single word for blue. There is goluboy, the light blue of a summer sky, and siniy, the dark blue of deep water — two colours, as separate to a Russian ear as pink and red are to an English one. And here is the strange part: in laboratory tests, Russian speakers are measurably faster at telling light blue from dark blue than English speakers are. The dictionary difference has become a difference in the eye. Where the language draws a line, its speakers learn to see one.',
                'In far northern Australia, the Kuuk Thaayorre people speak a language with no words for left and right. Everything is compass: there is an ant on your south-west leg; move the cup to the north-north-east. Even hello is directional — you greet a person by saying which way you are heading, which means that to speak at all you must always know. And so, from childhood, they always do: small children who have never seen a map can point toward home across a hundred kilometres of featureless bush. The grammar made the compass, and the compass became a sense.',
                'None of this makes a language a prison; anything can be said in any tongue, with enough patience. But each language is a habit of attention — a list of things its speakers are required to notice, be it gender, direction, or the exact blue of the sea. To learn a second one, then, is not merely to relabel the world you already have. It is to be handed a second pair of eyes, and to discover, mildly insulted, how much the first pair had agreed not to see.',
            ],
