            body: [
                'On the morning of September 28, 1941 — the last day of the season — Ted Williams’s batting average stood at .39955. Rounded, as the record book rounds, that is .400: a figure no hitter had reached in over a decade even then. His manager, Joe Cronin, offered him the obvious gift: sit out the day’s doubleheader in Philadelphia, and the .400 season was mathematically safe, forever. Williams was twenty-three years old. Nobody would have blamed him.',
                'He refused. “If I can’t hit .400 all the way,” he said, “I don’t deserve it.” Then he played both games of the doubleheader, went six-for-eight against the Athletics, and raised the number he had been invited to protect: .406. In all the seasons since — more than eighty of them — no player in the major leagues has hit .400 again. Not one. The record he would not shelter turned out to be the very performance nobody could repeat.',
                'The arithmetic difference between .39955 and .406 is a rounding error. The difference between rounding up and earning up is the entire story, and it is why the tale gets retold every September. Williams was offered the one deal loss aversion always offers — protect what you have, and never find out — and he declined it in the plainest terms available: he played. Six hits in eight tries later, the record was no longer something he was holding. It was something he had done.',
            ],
