            headline: 'The Stranger You Will Become',
            standfirst: 'We can all see how much we have changed. Almost none of us believe we will change again. Psychology’s quiet joke is that at every age we mistake the person we are now for the final one.',
            byline: 'By the Science Desk',
            body: [
                'Ask people how much they have changed in the last ten years, and they will tell you a great deal — different tastes, different friends, a self they can hardly recognise. Ask the same people how much they will change in the next ten, and they will say: hardly at all. At every age we feel we have just now, finally, become the finished version of ourselves. At every age, we are wrong.',
                'Researchers call it the end-of-history illusion, and it does not spare the wise. The music you will love, the work that will matter to you, the very values you would today call permanent — all of it will drift, as it always has, and you will not see it coming. We are far better historians of our past than prophets of our future, and we plan our lives as though the person arriving to live them will be exactly the one who set out.',
                'This is not a loss but a strange freedom. The regret you cannot imagine surviving, you will one day barely recall; the certainty you would stake your life on, you may gently outgrow. Hold today’s self a little more lightly, then, and leave a door open for the stranger you are becoming. He is on his way, this future you — and if the past is any guide, he will be glad you did not build the walls too high.',
            ],
