        lead: {
            kicker: 'THE BIG READ · IDEAS & MIND',
            headline: 'The Power of the Deadline',
            standfirst: 'Give a task a week and it swells to fill the week; give it an hour and, somehow, it is done. The deadline is not the enemy of good work. Very often it is the only reason the work exists at all.',
            byline: 'By the Ideas Desk',
            body: [
                'Work expands to fill the time you give it. The essay due in a month is begun in the final week; the same essay due on Friday is somehow finished by Friday. We treat the deadline as a cruelty imposed from outside, but it is closer to a kindness: it is the wall that turns a boundless, terrifying openness into a task a human being can actually start. Without an edge, the page stays blank forever.',
                'The reason is that unlimited time is not freedom but paralysis. Faced with forever, we do nothing, because nothing must be done yet; the work has no shape because it has no end. A constraint gives it one. The deadline, the word limit, the small budget — these are not obstacles to creativity but the banks that give the river its force. Remove them and you do not get a flood. You get a swamp.',
                'The practical lesson is to make deadlines even where none exist. Cut the open task into pieces and give each piece an hour and an end. Tell someone you will show them the thing on Tuesday, and let the small dread of Tuesday do for you what your good intentions never will. The finished, flawed, actual piece of work beats the perfect one you are still, in freedom, not making.',
            ],
        },
        rail: {
            kicker: 'THE DIAMOND · BASEBALL',
            headline: 'The Clock That Changed the Game',
            byline: 'By the Bullpen Desk',
            body: [
                'For a century baseball was the one game with no clock, a pastime that could, in theory, last forever. Then the sport looked at its own slowing pulse — the endless fidgeting, the stepped-off mound, the batter re-fastening gloves he had not unfastened — and did the unthinkable. It put a clock on the timeless game. The pitcher now has seconds, counted down on a board, to throw or be penalised.',
                'The purists mourned, and then a strange thing happened: the game got better. Freed of its own dithering, it grew tauter, quicker, more alive; the same nine innings that had once sprawled toward midnight now snapped along with purpose. It turned out the endless time had not been freedom. It had been an excuse — for delay, for fuss, for every small avoidance that a firm edge simply forbids.',
                'There is a lesson in the ballpark clock for anyone who has mistaken having no deadline for having no problem. The constraint did not ruin the game; it rescued it. Given all the time in the world, the players had used it to do less and less. Given a countdown, they did the thing they were there to do. Sometimes the kindest gift you can give a task is a clock ticking down beside it.',
            ],
        },
