                'He should not have read it. He read it on the walk home, under a streetlight, because he could not wait.',
                'It was not a love letter. It was a warning. Something had frightened her — she did not say what, only that she could not stay, could not write plainly, could not risk the letters arriving and being read by the wrong eyes. I am sending nothing until I am safe, she had written. If I go quiet, do not come looking for me here. And then, at the very bottom, in a rushed hand: If you don’t hear from me by Friday, go to—',
