                'The walk buys him time, but time is not really the point. The point is the break — the deliberate cutting of the thread between the last pitch and the next. A rattled pitcher who rushes only compounds the damage, throwing the next ball in the mood of the one before. The circle around the mound is a way of putting the previous moment down and refusing to carry it into this one.',
                'Every trade has its version of that slow circle: the breath before the reply, the walk to the kettle, the night’s sleep between the insult and the answer. The pitcher’s ritual is only the most visible. It teaches a plain and useful truth — that the space between two moments is yours to lengthen, and that almost nothing good was ever done in a hurry, on the mound or off it.',
