            headline: 'The Vanishing Art of Silence',
            standfirst: 'We have filled every gap with a voice or a song and called it company. It is worth asking what we are all so eager to drown out.',
            byline: 'By the Culture Desk',
            body: [
                'True silence has become one of the modern world’s rarest commodities. We have filled every gap — the lift, the queue, the walk to the station — with a voice or a song piped straight into the ear, and we call the result company. It is worth asking what we are so eager to drown out.',
                'For silence is not empty; it is where the mind finally hears itself. In the absence of input, the brain does not idle but turns inward — sorting the day, surfacing the buried thought, doing the slow work that noise forever interrupts. A culture that fears quiet is a culture that has stopped listening to its own interior, and cuts off every idea a moment before it can finish forming.',
                'The remedy costs nothing and feels, at first, like withdrawal. Leave the earphones in your pocket for one walk. Sit in a room and add nothing to it. The discomfort that rises is the sound of a mind unused to its own company — and if you can bear it a little longer, it settles, and begins, at last, to speak.',
            ],
