                'The changeup is a lie told with the truth’s own accent. Thrown with the violent arm-speed of a fastball, it leaves the hand looking like heat — and then arrives late, soft, a full gear slower, while the hitter, fooled by the honest motion, has already committed his swing to a ball that is not yet there.',
                'Its whole art is sameness. A changeup that looks different is worthless; the pitch must be identical to the fastball in everything but speed, so that the eye, reading the arm, tells the bat a confident lie. The deception lives not in the ball but in the matching — in the pitcher’s refusal to give the smallest thing away.',
                'It is the rare weapon that works by holding something back. The hard thrower who learns it late is often reborn, discovering that the pitch which beats the best hitters is not his fastest but his most disguised. Sometimes power is not the thing you add. It is the thing you conceal.',
