                'Halloran climbed back toward the dying light with the ledger under his arm, and this time he read it — not the last page, but the first, its ink browned with a century and a half of damp.',
                'The hand was different, older, but the words were the same words: “Kept the light. He came. Told him: not yet.” The first keeper had been a man named Eachann, who came to the island in 1871 to tend a new lamp, and who wrote, in a steadier moment, the only explanation the ledger ever gave. Halloran read it twice by the failing beam.',
                '“There is a thing in the water that wants to come ashore,” Eachann had written. “It cannot, while the light is kept and the keeper answers not yet. It offers each of us the one we have lost, to make us open the door. I have refused it forty years. I am tired. God forgive the man who tires before me.”',
                'Forty years of not yet. Then Eachann’s hand vanished from the pages, and another took it up, and another — each keeper inheriting the refusal like a debt that could be paid down but never cleared. Until tonight, when Halloran had spoken his brother’s name and begun, without knowing it, to open the very door the light was lit to hold.',
