                'Base hits that had fallen safely for a century now died in a fielder’s glove, standing precisely where the spreadsheet had sent him. Rallies withered. The sport grew quieter, cleverer, and duller. Efficiency had solved the game a little too well.',
                'So baseball did something rare: it legislated against its own intelligence, and drew lines the infielders may no longer cross. Purists howled about tradition; but the game remembered that it is, before it is a math problem, a show. Sometimes the right move is to forbid the optimal one.',
