                'Halloran did not say yes. Nor did he say the words the keepers had hidden behind for a hundred years — not yet — the small cowardice that had kept his brother standing in the dark, dripping, patient, unanswered.',
                'He said the boy’s name. Just that. “Tom.” It had been thirty years since he had let the word out of his mouth, and it came now cracked and quiet, the way a door long painted shut finally gives. On the stair the boy lifted his head, and something in his face — some question he had carried up out of the sea — went still at last.',
                'The voice, when it came, had lost its certainty. “That is not the bargain,” it said. “The bargain is yes, or not yet. There is no third word.” “There is now,” said Halloran. “I am not sending him back, and I am not keeping him waiting. I am coming down.”',
                'He set the ledger on the desk, open, and for the first time in eleven years he did not close it with those three cowardly words. He wrote something else. Then he took the stairs down into the dark where his brother waited — and behind him, untended for the first time since it died, the dead lamp of the tower began, slowly and then not slowly at all, to dim.',
