                'Ask anyone past forty and they will tell you the same thing: the years have begun to gather speed. A childhood summer was a country you could get lost in; a recent one is a receipt you barely remember signing. We are inclined to dismiss this as mere nostalgia, the old romanticising the young. But the sensation is real, measurable, and shared across nearly every culture that has thought to ask.',
                'Two explanations compete, and both are probably right. The first is arithmetic: to a child of five, a year is a fifth of everything; to a person of fifty, it is a rounding error. Each passing year is a thinner slice of the whole and feels, proportionally, briefer. The second is subtler and more hopeful. The brain does not clock time with a stopwatch; it estimates it afterwards, by the density of what it managed to record. Novelty lays down memory in thick, vivid layers; routine lays down almost nothing at all. A month of new faces and strange streets swells in the recollection, while a month of the familiar commute collapses to a single grey smear.',
                'This is why the mind plays its cruellest trick precisely as life grows most settled. The job we have mastered, the road we could drive asleep, the friends we have known for decades — comfort, all of it, and each a small theft of time. But the same mechanism offers a way out. Take the unfamiliar route home. Learn the difficult thing. Say yes to the trip you would rather skip. You cannot add hours to the clock, but you can thicken them — and a year you actually remember is, in the only sense that matters, a longer one.',
