            kicker: 'THE GROUND YOU WALK ON · RYOGOKU',
            headline: 'Thirty-Eight Thousand People Died on One City Block. Today It Is a Quiet Park.',
            standfirst:
                'Eight minutes from the office, past the sumo arena, there is a park where office workers eat lunch. On 1 September 1923 it was an empty lot that forty thousand people ran to, and a firestorm found them there.',
            byline: 'By the Ryogoku Bureau',
            body: [
                'Start with the correction, because the numbers matter and I had them wrong out loud. It was not 1910, and it was not before the First World War. The Great Kanto Earthquake struck at 11:58 in the morning on 1 September 1923 — five years after that war ended, and at the exact hour when every household in the city had a cooking fire lit for lunch. That timing is the whole disaster. The shaking did not kill most of the dead. The lunch fires did.',
                'What is now Yokoamicho Park was then the site of a former Army Clothing Depot: a large, cleared, empty lot in a district packed with wooden houses. It was the obvious place to run. Roughly forty thousand people ran there, dragging carts and bedding and everything they owned, and the carts and the bedding were the fuel. As the fires closed in from every direction, the heat generated its own weather — a fire whirl, a rotating column of flame — and it came through the lot.',
                'About thirty-eight thousand people died on that one piece of ground, in something close to fifteen minutes. Not hundreds. Thirty-eight thousand, in a space you can walk across in three minutes today. It remains the single deadliest incident of the earthquake, and one of the deadliest city blocks in the history of any city.',
                'Seven years later, in 1930, a memorial hall was raised on the spot to hold the unidentified remains. It was designed by Chuta Ito, an architect who put Indian and Chinese elements into a building that reads, at a glance, like a temple. In 1951 it was renamed and rededicated to hold something else as well: the dead of the 1945 Tokyo air raids. It now holds roughly one hundred and sixty-three thousand sets of remains from both — a natural disaster and a man-made one, filed under one roof.',
                'There is a second stone in the park, smaller, off to one side, put up in 1973 on the fiftieth anniversary. In the days after the earthquake, rumours spread that Korean residents were poisoning wells and setting fires. They were not true. Soldiers, police and civilian vigilante groups killed Korean and Chinese residents across the city. The number is still disputed, and it has been politically contested for a century, which is itself part of the story. There is a memorial ceremony at that stone every 1 September, and in recent years there have been counter-demonstrators at it. A hundred years is not long enough.',
                'None of this is signposted from the station. There is no queue. Twenty metres away the sumo arena fills up, the Hokusai museum takes coach parties, and the sword museum sits inside a tidal garden that once belonged to a feudal lord. The park is free, shaded, and mostly empty. Office workers cross it at speed with their phones up. That is not disrespect. It is exactly what the place was built to allow.',
            ],
