            headline: 'Boredom Titan Breaches the Wall — Fluency Escapes on the Other Side',
            standfirst:
                'A routine English lesson was being overrun by small talk. Then a single word — "titan" — tore the wall open, and every sentence that had refused to come all evening came pouring out at once.',
            byline: 'Filed from the Shiganshina Bureau',
            body: [
                'For the first thirty minutes, the front held nothing. The enemy came in the usual disguise: the office Wi-Fi went down. Our soldier opened his mouth, reached for the words, and got as far as "it is damaging our—" before the sentence collapsed under him. Damaging our what? Even he no longer knew. The weather advanced. The weekend advanced. He fell back, position after position, saying almost nothing and meaning even less.',
                'Then the teacher, half by accident, asked about anime. And the moment the words "Attack on Titan" left her mouth, something enormous came over the wall.',
                'He did not stop talking. The plot, the animation, MAPPA, the grown men on the internet crying at reaction videos — it all came firing out of the exact same mouth that, ninety seconds earlier, had died on "damaging our—". The vocabulary was there. The grammar mostly held. The teacher started laughing, because she could see it too: this was a completely different soldier.',
                'And here is the dispatch worth printing in red. It was never a wall of grammar. It was a wall of desire. Boring topics do not fail us because our English is weak; they fail us because we would not say them in Japanese either. There is nothing behind them to push the words out. Light a topic you would run toward, and even broken English charges the field. Leave the topic cold, and no amount of correct grammar will get a single word to move.',
                'The lesson, in one line: stop trying to be fluent in things you do not care about. Find the one titan you would gladly go over the wall for, and speak from there. Everything else is just standing on the ramparts, mouth open, saying "damaging our—" to the fog.',
            ],
